booktitle={2025 Artificial Intelligence x Humanities, Education, and Art (AIxHEART)}, 
title={Generative AI as a Pedagogical Agent:Investigating Student Reliance, Perceived Utility, and Ethical Trade-Offs in Computer Science Education}, 
year={2025},
volume={},
number={},
pages={1-6},
abstract={This study is concerned with the role of Large Language Models (LLMs) in Higher Education Institutes (HEIs) curricula regarding computer science students' perceptions and attitudes. In the subsection of the humanities and STEM, new challenges have risen through the widespread availability of ChatGPT from data gathered. In an analysis of 165 participants in a structured questionnaire designed to measure key variables such as ChatGPT usage frequency, perceived effectiveness/ineffectiveness, and concerns about overreliance, ethical dilemmas, and career readiness. Utilizing these variables, we explored concerns regarding the pedagogical shift in CS curriculum and possible ways to circumvent issues by taking into account students' perceptions. Findings help give a more nuanced understanding of the motivational factors at play for CS students and prospects they may have which may not be fulfilled in the current space. This study also serves to inform future policymakers and HEIs on guidelines for universities that are similarly facing the same ethical issues of data privacy, bias, student autonomy, and more. Finally, this study sheds light on new areas of research in the interdisciplinary humanities and CS to tackle education inequality, ethical questions, motivation, and ever-changing pedagogical paradigms.},
