pages={1-6},
abstract={The growing demand for personalized education has driven the development of adaptive learning systems, particularly in technical fields such as object-oriented programming. This study presents the design and evaluation of an educational chatbot that uses artificial intelligence and natural language processing to improve student performance in the topics of Classes and Inheritance. Utilizing Dialogflow CX for conversational flows, Google Cloud Functions for real-time logic, and Firebase for data storage, the chatbot employs a Naive Bayes classifier that dynamically adapts questions based on student answers, response time, and feedback requests. A controlled experiment with 115 students, randomly assigned to Naive Bayes and Item Response Theory (IRT) groups, measured the improvement in the correct answer rate between initial and subsequent assessments. Results showed an average improvement of 2.8% for the Naive Bayes group compared to 1.2% for the IRT group, demonstrating greater adaptability of the Bayesian approach. This work presents a scalable and replicable framework for adaptive learning, with practical implications for enhancing engagement and retention in programming education. However, further optimization is needed for session duration and cognitive load.},
